mbar - minimal status bar for mango
===================================
Tags on the left, dwmblocks-style blocks on the right. Docks to the
top edge via layer-shell. Gruvbox dark. UTF-8 aware.

Single C file. Depends on libwayland-client only. Two text renderers,
chosen at compile time:

  default    anti-aliased TTF via the bundled public-domain
             stb_truetype, with font fallbacks: nerd font icons
             (tinted) and full-color emoji (joypixels, NotoColorEmoji;
             CBDT pngs decoded by the bundled stb_image). No gtk,
             cairo, pango or freetype.
  BITMAP=1   embedded public-domain 8x8 pixel font, ascii only.
             Needs no font files at all.

Requirements
------------
wayland (libwayland-client + wayland-scanner), a C compiler, make.
Protocol XMLs are vendored in protocols/.

Installation
------------
    make                # ttf renderer
    make BITMAP=1       # pixel font renderer
    sudo make install

The first build copies config.def.h to config.h. Run mbar from your
mango autostart.

Configuration
-------------
mbar is configured by editing config.h and (re)compiling, like dwm:
colors ("#rrggbb" or "#aarrggbb"), bar height, font size, separator,
padding, fonts and blocks all live there. config.h is yours; it is
never overwritten and survives make clean. Delete it to start over
from config.def.h. When switching between ttf and BITMAP=1 builds,
run make clean first.

Fonts (ttf build): the first existing path in fontpaths[] is the
primary font; fallbackpaths[] are searched for codepoints the primary
lacks. Nerd font symbols render tinted with FG; color emoji fonts
(joypixels, NotoColorEmoji) render in full color. Emoji are drawn per
codepoint: ZWJ sequences and flags show as their components, skin
tone modifiers as swatches. Override the primary without recompiling:

    MBAR_FONT=/path/to/font.ttf mbar

Tags: current = block, urgent = red, hidden = gray. Event-driven via
ext-workspace-v1.

Blocks
------
A block is a function that writes a string. Define it in config.h
and add it to blocks[]:

    static void blk_bat(char *o, size_t n) {
            FILE *f = fopen("/sys/class/power_supply/BAT0/capacity", "r");
            int c = 0; if (f) { fscanf(f, "%d", &c); fclose(f); }
            snprintf(o, n, "BAT %d%%", c);
    }
    static const struct block blocks[] = {
            { blk_bat,  NULL },
            { blk_date, NULL },
            { blk_time, "foot -e sh -c 'cal -3; read _'" },
    };

Each block may carry a click command, run detached on left click
(terminal programs need a terminal, e.g. "foot -e nmtui"). Clicking
a workspace tag switches to it.

For scripts, put them in modules/ and use blk_cmd with a per-block
cache and interval:

    static void blk_vol(char *o, size_t n) {
            static struct cmdcache c;
            blk_cmd("volume.sh", 2, &c, o, n);
    }

Everything redraws once per second; blk_cmd runs its command every
N seconds and serves the cached first line in between.

Modules
-------
Every file in modules/ is embedded into the binary at build time
(gen-modules.sh turns them into C strings in modules.h). At runtime
blk_cmd("name.sh", ...) runs the embedded copy through sh; the binary
is fully self-contained and needs nothing installed. A name with no
match in modules/ is run as an ordinary shell command instead, so
blk_cmd("date +%V", ...) and absolute script paths still work.

Editing a module requires a rebuild; make notices modules/ changed
and regenerates. Modules run under sh, so shebangs are ignored:
write POSIX sh, or wrap other interpreters (python3 <<'PY' ... PY).
